# what is in them

Four graphite and ink drawings. Enormous horned brutes, and the drawing itself is
doing as much work as the subject.

**They are monochrome.** Graphite grey, paper white, ink black, and the whole
tonal range in between worked with crosshatch and smudge. Not stylised flat
shading -- rendered, heavily, the way a pencil renders when somebody has spent a
long time on it.

**With exactly one colour, and it is blood.** In the third drawing the only red on
the page is what is coming off the flail and what is pooling under the body on the
ground. Nothing else in the frame is coloured at all. That restraint is the whole
effect: a monochrome page with one red in it does not read as a black-and-white
picture, it reads as a picture where **the only thing worth colouring is the
killing**.

**They are covered in what they have killed.** Skulls hung off the belt, tusks,
trophies lashed to the arms. Not decoration -- a record.

**They stand over things.** Two of the four are mid-stride across a body. The
posture is not a fighting stance; it is the moment after.

**Knotwork.** Carved stone behind one of them, deep-relief interlace. Somebody
built that wall and it was a long time ago.

--

# what this project should take from it

**The monsters are not a third team colour. They are the absence of one.**

Both sides of this game are colour -- one warm, one cold -- and that is the whole
visual read of the map. A monster drawn in a third hue is a third faction. A
monster drawn in **bone and graphite**, with no team colour anywhere on it, is
something that does not belong to the colour system at all, which is exactly what a
body on nobody's side should look like.

**And one red accent, used for nothing else in the game.** Not a health bar, not a
refusal, not a warning. Red belongs to the monsters, so that the first time a
player sees it they know what it means without being told.

**The trophies are a rendering idea for later**, when there are sprites rather than
discs: what a monster carries could be what it has killed this match. Nothing needs
that now and it would be the wrong thing to build first, but it is the kind of
detail that pays for itself, because it makes the second challenge look different
from the first without anybody drawing a second monster.

--

# what it does not settle

The setting's visual language is still open -- see issue 702. This says what the
things out of the middle look like. It does not say what the towers look like, or
the bodies, or the ground, and it should not be stretched into saying so: an
automated war nobody started is a colder image than these drawings, and the two
have to be reconciled by somebody rather than assumed to agree.

The one thing it does argue for beyond the monsters: **this world is drawn, not
rendered.** Hatching, weight, the pencil visible. That is a long way from what is
on screen now, which is discs on a dark field, and it is worth knowing that the
discs are a placeholder for something with a hand in it rather than the intended
end state.
